WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.759
- Bro, do you know how many
people watching right now

00:00:01.759 --> 00:00:03.813
were scared to ask out the person

00:00:03.813 --> 00:00:05.980
that they were most attracted to

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.660
and thus missed out on the opportunity

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.720
of marrying the person
that they most were in to?

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.553
That's life.

00:00:10.553 --> 00:00:11.970
You don't ask, you don't get.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.427
You got your perspective.

00:00:15.427 --> 00:00:16.785
(engines roaring)

00:00:16.785 --> 00:00:18.786
(crowd cheering)

00:00:18.786 --> 00:00:19.619
I just wanna be happy.

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:20.672
Don't you wanna be happy?

00:00:20.672 --> 00:00:23.422
(slow R&amp;B music)

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:28.463
("Rush Hour" by Ely)

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.930
We're on the way to this
fireside chat with Ken Auletta.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.310
It's advertising week here in New York

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.360
which means there are thousands of events

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:50.410
for the ad world going
on throughout the week.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.170
Bunch of our leaders are
doing a bunch of events

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:53.805
and I'm doing one as well.

00:00:53.805 --> 00:00:55.804
To give you macro context vlog,

00:00:55.804 --> 00:00:57.690
we have a new person filming.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.367
Why don't you say hello?

00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:01.090
- Hello (laughs)

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:01.923
- [Gary] What's your name?

00:01:01.923 --> 00:01:02.756
- Josiah.

00:01:02.756 --> 00:01:03.759
- [Gary] And Josiah,
you excited to be here?

00:01:03.759 --> 00:01:05.650
- Yes, more than anything. (laughs)

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.530
- [Gary] Good, so Josiah's excited.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:08.670
I'm only here on Monday,

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:10.770
the Jets lost to the Jags yesterday

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:11.603
so I'm super pissed.

00:01:11.603 --> 00:01:12.781
Congrats to Jacksonville.

00:01:12.781 --> 00:01:14.400
I'm only here today.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.950
I'm in London the rest of the week

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:19.180
so I'm trying to cram an
entire week into a day.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.023
So I'll be rolling until midnight tonight.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.020
Now, I'm running to do this quick event

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.120
and so that's where I'm at at this point.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.920
This event will probably
be the meat of this episode

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.928
because you haven't been
able to film quite a bit yet

00:01:31.928 --> 00:01:33.750
'cause so many internal meetings,

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.310
just operating the business,

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:37.470
one of the vulnerabilities of the vlog is

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.850
I talk about being the
entrepreneurial CEO operator

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:42.953
of my companies but
you don't get to see it

00:01:42.953 --> 00:01:45.780
because so much of it is
sensitive information.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.490
You're getting a first-hand look day one

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:51.622
of how much can't be filmed
but that's where we're at.

00:01:51.622 --> 00:01:54.705
("Rush Hour" by Ely)

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.108
- [Gary] Yeah, no worries.

00:02:09.108 --> 00:02:10.420
- [Leonard] My name is Leonard Armato

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:11.253
- Hey Leonard.

00:02:11.253 --> 00:02:12.660
- A friend of mine
represented Shaquille O'Neal

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:13.493
for many years.

00:02:13.493 --> 00:02:14.326
- Amazing.

00:02:14.326 --> 00:02:15.159
- And has been really
good friends with him.

00:02:15.159 --> 00:02:15.992
- Amazing.

00:02:15.992 --> 00:02:16.890
- I watch your stuff.
- Thank you, Leonard.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.240
- I don't know how you have the time

00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:21.260
to do all those videos
everyday, how do you do it?

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:22.610
- It's just prioritizing.

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:26.563
To me, especially being
in the creative field,

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.680
I think being a practitioner of the craft

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:31.780
that you're trying to
get people to understand

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:32.613
is powerful.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:35.100
I didn't grow up in it,
you definitely didn't

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:38.360
and yet, we couldn't do
the same things Apple did,

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.010
or Nike did as individuals, but now,

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.830
with distribution being commoditized

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:45.217
or changing, I think it's important

00:02:45.217 --> 00:02:47.060
for me to understand the context

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:49.980
of creating and distributing
content with an agenda.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.150
No different then what I
would require, want, target,

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:57.350
and so I prioritize it is the real answer.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:00.380
I think people still view it as secondary

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:05.380
or nice to have and I believe
that a lot of the people here,

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:08.520
for all of us, the vulnerability becomes

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.340
when you get so far away from the reality

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:13.233
of the end-consumer we're
sitting in an ivory tower.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.550
I grew up in using
marketing and advertising

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:18.770
to build a family business.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:19.603
- I saw.

00:03:19.603 --> 00:03:22.094
- For me, the way I view it is,

00:03:22.094 --> 00:03:24.450
unless I'm putting in the
time to actually create,

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:28.840
distribute, write copy, edit, think,

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:30.900
then I'm vulnerable to being the thing

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.970
that I think is the most
vulnerable in the ad world now,

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:35.100
which is a lack of understanding

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.120
what's actually being
consumed by the end consumer.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.360
So, it takes a lot of time, but to me,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:45.060
there's three meetings I'm
probably having every weekday

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.316
that are less important
than I think they are.

00:03:47.316 --> 00:03:48.149
- Yeah.

00:03:48.149 --> 00:03:49.870
- That if I can allocate
that to content creation

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:51.960
and distribution and understanding
of the end-consumer's

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:55.130
thoughts will make me better
in the other 19 meetings

00:03:55.130 --> 00:03:57.600
I'm in that are meaningful
with a point of view,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:59.310
a strategy and understanding.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:02.393
("Rush Hour" by Ely)

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:07.070
- Fifteen, twenty years
ago, you know or so,

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:08.940
advertising told you
what you're gonna wear,

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:09.773
- Yes.

00:04:09.773 --> 00:04:10.606
- What you're gonna eat,
- Yes.

00:04:10.606 --> 00:04:11.439
- Where you're gonna vacation
- Yes.

00:04:11.439 --> 00:04:12.272
- And what car you're gonna drive.

00:04:12.272 --> 00:04:13.105
- Yes.

00:04:13.105 --> 00:04:15.300
- 2018, with all these movements going on,

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.337
all this spiritual going on, it's changed.

00:04:18.337 --> 00:04:20.161
Now, the advertisers are reacting.

00:04:20.161 --> 00:04:20.994
- Yes.

00:04:20.994 --> 00:04:23.181
- You, as a key guy in
the middle of all that,

00:04:23.181 --> 00:04:24.014
- Yes.

00:04:24.014 --> 00:04:24.847
- are a great guy to talk
to about all of that.

00:04:24.847 --> 00:04:25.680
- Thank you.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:26.513
You know, I'm gonna be honest with you,

00:04:26.513 --> 00:04:29.414
I think if you go back
to 1960s or the 80s,

00:04:29.414 --> 00:04:32.600
a company, an advertiser, or a brand

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.600
that was advertising was doing it right,

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.920
they also were reacting AKA listening.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.410
I think the difference today, you, I, him,

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.320
everybody watching, well,
they can make the content

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:45.570
that we listen to, so it's more visual,

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:48.400
but if you go back to ,
"Just Do It" or "Priceless"

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:51.020
or "Where's the Beef"
or the Jolly Green Giant

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:54.470
the strategy and insights
that led to the creative

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:55.410
were imperative.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:58.038
They did it in focus groups,
they did it a different way,

00:04:58.038 --> 00:05:02.140
but I would always say the
best talking AKA advertising

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.640
was always on the back
of the best listening.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:06.800
I just think that all
of us can see the way

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:08.090
that brands are listening now

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:11.160
cause it's in all our faces
and everybody's producing.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.580
- Talk to me about how
Hollywood, athletes,

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.650
all different walks of
life are coming together

00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:18.610
and their voices now are being heard

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:20.320
where the advertisers are listening,

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.743
as you saw with the Chase spot with

00:05:21.743 --> 00:05:22.890
the Serena and the US Open.

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.780
- I think the biggest difference today,

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:28.070
than let's say, Babe Ruth or
Mickey Mantel or Joe Montana

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:32.510
days is humans can now,
celebrities or any human,

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:34.863
can go direct to the
consumer and they don't need

00:05:34.863 --> 00:05:38.170
an editor in the middle, they
don't need Walter Cronkite

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:40.440
in the middle, they don't
need ESPN in the middle

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.630
and what that's doing is
changing the distribution

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.380
and message dynamics in
a way we've never seen.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:50.360
So, yes, whether you're
Beyonce, or Serena, or LeBron,

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:54.854
or a politician or an actress
on a hit Netflix show,

00:05:54.854 --> 00:05:57.920
you've got leverage now
that we've never seen before

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.810
because of Instagram,
Facebook, YouTube and Podcasts

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:02.390
going directly to the consumer,

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:07.250
not having a middle man filter
you, stop you, or package you

00:06:07.250 --> 00:06:08.404
in the way that they want.

00:06:08.404 --> 00:06:11.487
("Rush Hour" by Ely)

00:06:14.841 --> 00:06:18.890
- Your thoughts on your
Podcasts, your videos,

00:06:18.890 --> 00:06:22.620
which I really like,
have given me what I need

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.391
to kind of take some of these chances.

00:06:25.391 --> 00:06:26.224
- Good.

00:06:26.224 --> 00:06:28.400
- I mean, I have a great
gig and I'm in a good place,

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:30.630
but I'm also trying to
feed that creative spirit.

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:32.740
I was an entrepreneur for many years.

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:34.790
- And to me, I'm sure you
must have picked up on this

00:06:34.790 --> 00:06:38.510
why it's resonating, it blows
me away how young I think

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:39.460
you and I are.

00:06:39.460 --> 00:06:40.293
- Yeah.

00:06:40.293 --> 00:06:41.126
- And I think.

00:06:41.126 --> 00:06:41.959
- I listen to that a lot from you.

00:06:41.959 --> 00:06:43.736
- And I think that is confusing
to you and I normally.

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:44.819
- Yeah.

00:06:44.819 --> 00:06:47.530
Cause we work the industry
with, but that's--

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:49.910
- I think it's important
and it makes me so happy

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:53.050
because it means a lot to me.

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:54.600
I just don't think, I don't know,

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.600
we've got 50 fucking more years.

00:06:56.600 --> 00:07:00.130
I don't know why people think
it's over or a tail-end.

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:01.370
And I get it, I know why,

00:07:01.370 --> 00:07:03.950
because we've been putting
in work for a long time,

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:06.655
but that makes me happy that it resonated.

00:07:06.655 --> 00:07:07.488
- Yeah.

00:07:08.388 --> 00:07:09.221
- Thank you.

00:07:09.221 --> 00:07:10.778
- I appreciate, man.

00:07:10.778 --> 00:07:13.861
("Rush Hour" by Ely)

00:07:15.210 --> 00:07:18.910
- One of the fasting growing ad platforms

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:20.830
is owned by Amazon.

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:21.782
- Yes.

00:07:21.782 --> 00:07:23.520
- Where do you see them in the future?

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.770
- Destroying everybody's face.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.349
(audience laughs)

00:07:27.349 --> 00:07:28.310
- Explain.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:29.830
- If the government doesn't stop Amazon,

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:32.139
it's disproportionately the
biggest company in the world.

00:07:32.139 --> 00:07:36.330
They are, the level of respect that I have

00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:39.550
for the merit of the
skillsets of their executions

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:42.560
and how Bezos has run
that company pissed me off

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:44.262
because I'm like, "Shit."

00:07:44.262 --> 00:07:47.790
I always rely on, "Well, I
still have 40 years to execute,

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:49.330
to do my thing."

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:52.220
Man, they have, it's unbelievable to me

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:56.100
how good of a company
that is and here's why

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.720
and it is absolutely why I think

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:00.450
any level of success I've had in my career

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:01.979
has been on a very similar principal.

00:08:01.979 --> 00:08:05.513
They're outrageously pro their customer.

00:08:06.453 --> 00:08:09.153
Amazon's winning because you love it.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:12.778
Prime is like oxygen.

00:08:12.778 --> 00:08:15.611
(audience laughs)

00:08:16.510 --> 00:08:18.460
Everybody buys everything on it

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:22.653
and then they layer
entertainment on top of it,

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.340
don't even want to get into
the fact that "Sawdust"

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.040
is one of my favorite business concepts,

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:30.190
which is selling a
by-product of what you do,

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:33.170
which they've done better than
anybody with Amazon services.

00:08:33.170 --> 00:08:34.870
They built all that
infrastructure for themselves,

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:36.340
then they sold it to the world.

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:38.540
I mean that company has
just done so much right,

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:39.750
it's uncomfortable.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.860
- You can make an argument
that no one has better data

00:08:42.860 --> 00:08:45.630
than they have because
they have the online search

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:47.154
which is what you're interested in

00:08:47.154 --> 00:08:50.320
or Facebook which you and your
friends were talking about,

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:52.030
they have ads which you purchase.

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.354
- Yeah, I mean, they are--

00:08:53.354 --> 00:08:56.590
- How do you see them using advertising?

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:59.300
- Easy, when that data goes to the web

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:03.172
and they share, sell,
or leverage that data,

00:09:03.172 --> 00:09:07.510
What's amazing about Google
is it's intent to buy.

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:09.660
What a lot of very
educated people here know

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.960
is that Google ads are extremely strong,

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:14.210
even against Facebook,
if you're talking about

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:17.100
sales intent in a
short-term, whereas Facebook

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.440
is crazy and why I've been
surprised the ad world

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:22.100
hasn't embraced it more, is building brand

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:23.370
on top of funnel.

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:26.630
People misunderstand
Facebook in our industry.

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:28.700
They think of it as more
of a direct-to-consumer

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:31.699
strength place, Facebook
is absolutely the platform

00:09:31.699 --> 00:09:35.610
that has replaced
television for the ambitions

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:36.900
we have for television,

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:40.880
which is creating above the
funnel, top brand awareness.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.428
What Amazon has is the cleanest data

00:09:43.428 --> 00:09:46.030
you can see about buying,
'cause it's not even intent.

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:49.030
I want to buy scissors,

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:50.720
on a Google search is a really good data

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:53.280
if you sell scissors and then
you put ads in front of them.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:56.120
But actual data that you bought scissors,

00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:00.030
that really matters and
so, when you think about,

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:02.900
their ambitions and I think
people are very naive.

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:05.490
I don't think people
understand what happens next.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.430
I think, for example, this is really wild

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:10.150
because I saw all the
branding, I'm convinced

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:13.530
that Target is in deep shit

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:17.610
and that they have no place
in the world of retail

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:20.530
and either Facebook or
Amazon will buy them, right?

00:10:20.530 --> 00:10:23.230
And so, I don't think people
realize the retail wars

00:10:23.230 --> 00:10:24.063
that are--

00:10:24.063 --> 00:10:24.896
- Why would they buy them?

00:10:24.896 --> 00:10:27.140
- Well, because what
Facebook has to understand

00:10:27.140 --> 00:10:29.000
because I think Cheryl
and Mark are super smart

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:31.780
and what Google has to
understand is that Amazon's

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:34.345
retail leverage is unbelievably important

00:10:34.345 --> 00:10:36.750
to the advertising world
that we all live in

00:10:36.750 --> 00:10:41.340
and I think I would be stunned
if Target isn't sold to

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:45.610
Facebook, Amazon, Google,
or maybe even Apple.

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:48.600
I would just be stunned
because it's a pillar

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.170
that the rest of them are vulnerable to.

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:54.073
To Amazon and it's data
and execution and leverage.

00:10:55.650 --> 00:10:56.483
- Thank you.

00:10:56.483 --> 00:10:57.316
- He's gonna be late.

00:10:57.316 --> 00:11:00.287
- Just want to get a quick
photo with you please.

00:11:03.043 --> 00:11:04.126
- Okay, Gary.

00:11:05.998 --> 00:11:09.498
(smooth, confident music)

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:14.313
- I'm so flattered that
people appreciate my work.

00:11:14.313 --> 00:11:15.146
- [Josiah] Mm-hmm.

00:11:15.146 --> 00:11:17.090
- I mean, I don't think
people, I mean, I will never,

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:19.400
to the day that I die take for granted

00:11:19.400 --> 00:11:21.050
that somebody wants to
take a selfie with me,

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:23.138
that is like, so flattering.

00:11:23.138 --> 00:11:25.990
You know, even hearing
